WEBVTT

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.940
Risk management connects cybersecurity activity to mission and operational outcomes.

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:09.110
Begin by defining the system, physical process, assets, dependencies, owners,

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:10.205
and boundaries.

00:00:10.205 --> 00:00:14.575
Identify what the organization must protect: safe operation, reliable control, production,

00:00:14.575 --> 00:00:18.180
environmental limits, service delivery, data, and recovery capability.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:23.050
Next, consider threats, vulnerabilities, likelihood, existing safeguards, and impact.

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:25.135
Threats may be malicious or accidental.

00:00:25.135 --> 00:00:28.796
Vulnerabilities may be technical, architectural, procedural, or human.

00:00:28.796 --> 00:00:32.590
Impact should include safety, equipment, quality, downtime, mission, environment,

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:35.775
financial loss, and public consequence where applicable.

00:00:35.775 --> 00:00:37.780
Operations and engineering teams are essential participants.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:42.040
They understand process states, maintenance constraints, independent safety functions,

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:45.255
and the consequences of losing visibility or control.

00:00:45.255 --> 00:00:49.110
Cybersecurity teams contribute threat knowledge, architecture analysis, access pathways,

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:51.095
monitoring, and incident experience.

00:00:51.095 --> 00:00:54.260
Risk decisions are stronger when both perspectives are present.

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:57.380
Control selection should reduce the most important risk without creating greater

00:00:57.380 --> 00:00:58.285
operational harm.

00:00:58.285 --> 00:01:01.795
Segmentation may reduce exposure while a patch awaits testing.

00:01:01.795 --> 00:01:05.460
Secure remote access may preserve vendor support while improving accountability.

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:08.700
Backup and recovery controls may reduce the duration of disruption.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:11.500
Each decision should state assumptions and evidence.

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:13.935
Residual risk remains after controls are applied.

00:01:13.935 --> 00:01:17.685
Accountable leadership must understand and accept that risk, fund additional treatment,

00:01:17.685 --> 00:01:21.660
avoid the activity, or transfer part of the exposure through appropriate arrangements.

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:25.380
Risk acceptance should not be an indefinite technical exception without ownership.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:29.155
Reassess when systems, connectivity, suppliers, threats, vulnerabilities,

00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:31.420
or process consequences change.

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:33.750
Risk management is a continuing operational cycle,

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:36.180
not a one-time document produced before commissioning.
